sentences.t:
Oídme toda la cort e pésevos de mio mal;
ifantes de Carrión, quem desondraron tan mal,
a menos de riebtos no los puedo dexar.

sentences.l:
Hear-me all the court and let-it-grieve-you of my wrong;
infantes of Carrión, who-me dishonoured so badly,
without challenges not them I-can leave.

sentences.t:
Vezós mio Çid a llas cortes pregonadas;
dexóla creçer e luenga trae la barba;
los unos le han miedo e los otros espanta.

sentences.l:
Accustomed-himself my Çid to the courts proclaimed;
let-it grow and long wears the beard;
the some of-him have fear and the others he-frightens.

for not me took by it, son of woman born,
nor-me-it plucked son of Moor nor of Christian-woman,
as I you, count, in the castle of Cabra.

sentences.t:
“Dexássedesvos Çid de aquesta razón;
de vuestros averes de todos pagado ssodes.
Non creciés varaja entre nos e vos.

sentences.l:
“Should-leave-off-you, Çid, from this matter;
of your goods of all paid you-are.
Not should-grow dispute between us and you.

sentences.t:
Per Vermu[doz] conpeçó de fablar;
detiénesle la lengua, non puede delibrar,
mas quando enpieça, sabed, nol da vagar:

sentences.l:
Per Vermu[doz] began to speak;
holds-itself-on-him the tongue, not he-can get-free,
but when he-begins, know-ye, not-it he-gives rest:

sentences.t:
pedist las feridas primeras al Canpeador leal,
vist un moro, fústel ensayar;
antes fuxiste que a [él] te allegasses.

sentences.l:
you-asked the blows first of-the Campeador loyal,
you-saw a Moor, you-went-him to-attack;
sooner you-fled than to [him] you-could-draw-near.

sentences.t:
de los primeros colpes ofle de arrancar;
did el cavallo, tóveldo en poridad:
fasta este día no lo descubrí a nadi.

sentences.l:
of the first blows I-had-him to rout;
I-gave-you the horse, I-kept-it-for-you in secret:
until this day not it I-revealed to anyone.

sentences.t:
Delant mio Çid e delante todos ovístete de alabar
que mataras el moro e que fizieras barnax;
croviérontelo todos, mas non saben la verdad.

sentences.l:
Before my Çid and before all you-had-yourself to praise
that you-had-killed the Moor and that you-had-done a-deed-of-prowess;
believed-you-it all, but not they-know the truth.
